EDGECLAW(1)                General Commands Manual                EDGECLAW(1)

NAME
     edgeclaw — AI-powered personal assistant agent with shell-like syntax

SYNOPSIS
     edgeclaw [--setup] [--sandbox] [-c CMD] [--help] [--version] [SCRIPT]

DESCRIPTION
     EdgeClaw is a command-line AI assistant offering bash-inspired task man-
     agement.  It supports multi-turn conversations, tool calling, pipeline
     chaining, I/O redirection, variable expansion, and Unix-style job con-
     trol for orchestrating multiple parallel agent tasks.

OPTIONS
     -s, --setup        Run initial setup wizard and exit.
     -S, --sandbox      Enable filesystem sandbox for tool execution.
     -c, --command CMD  Execute a single command and exit (non-interactive).
     -h, --help         Display help and exit.
     -V, --version      Output version information and exit.

     If a file path is given as the final argument and it is not an option,
     edgeclaw executes each non-empty, non-comment line as a command
     (shell script mode), then exits.

GETTING STARTED
           $ edgeclaw
           EdgeClaw CLI ready.  Type /help for commands.
           > explain this code
           [task] created 1 (group 1)
           ...

     Type and press Enter.  All non-/ input creates a task in the current
     foreground group and sends it to the LLM.  Conversations are saved per
     session under workspace/<session_id>/.

     Tab completion is available for commands (type / then Tab) and will
     cycle through matching completions on subsequent Tab presses.

SHELL SYNTAX
     All non-/ input is processed through the edgeclaw shell parser:

     Implicit task creation
           > analyze src/ for bugs
       Creates a task in the current foreground group with the text as input.

     @ syntax (new group)
           > @review the latest commits
       Same as above but creates a new group for the task.

     Pipe (|)
           > scan for issues | fix them | write a report
       Each segment is a separate task, labeled [pipe].  Each task depends
       on the previous one.  When a task completes, its output is injected
       into the next task's LLM context.

     Sequential (;)
           > review src/ ; run tests ; summarize > report.md
       Splits on ; (outside quotes) and executes each sub-command in order.
       Each segment is labeled [seq].  Tasks wait for the previous one to
       complete, but output is NOT injected between segments (unlike |).

     Background (&)
           > run the full test suite &
       Runs the task in the background.  The prompt returns immediately.

     Output redirect (>  >>  2>)
           > analyze code > report.md
           > append log >> all.log
           > build 2> errors.log
       After the task completes, its output is written to the specified file.
       >> appends.  2> writes stderr.  Quoted > is not interpreted.

     Input redirect (<)
           > fix bugs < context.txt
       Reads context.txt and appends its content to the task's user input
       (if there is preceding text, it is joined with a newline separator).
       If the file does not exist or is a directory, the task is NOT created
       and an error message is shown instead.

     Variable expansion ($)
           > $LAST
           > $1
           > $$
       $LAST  — output of the most recently completed task (by completion
                order, not creation order).
       $N     — output of task with id N (e.g. $1, $2).
       $$     — task id of the current foreground active task.
       Expansion happens after pipe splitting and redirect stripping, so
       any | or > in the expanded output is treated as literal text.

     History expansion (!)
           > !!
           > !3
       !!  — repeat the last command.
       !n  — repeat the nth command from history (see /history).

     Options on any shell command
           > "input text" --bg -d task1 --group dev --prompt reviewer
       Options must appear after the input or be the only content:
       --bg              Run in background.
       -d <id>           Wait for this task before executing.
       --group <id>      Create in this group (-g is a shortcut).
       --prompt <name>   Use this subagent prompt template.

SHELL ARCHITECTURE
     Input processing follows a Unix-style pipeline:

       User Input → History Expansion → Tokenizer → Parser → AST → Executor

     Tokenizer
       A quote-aware tokenizer respecting " and ' quoting.  Token types:
       TOK_TEXT (regular text), TOK_PIPE (|), TOK_SEQ (;), TOK_BG (&),
       TOK_REDIR_OUT (>), TOK_REDIR_APPEND (>>), TOK_REDIR_ERR (2>),
       TOK_REDIR_IN (<).

     Parser & AST
       Recursive-descent parser building an AST (Abstract Syntax Tree):

         AST_ROOT   — top-level wrapper
           AST_SEQ  — ; chain (sequential dependency)
             AST_PIPE — | chain (pipe dependency with injection)
               AST_BG     — & background modifier
               AST_REDIR_* — > / >> / 2> / < redirection
               AST_CMD   — leaf node: text + options

       The executor walks the tree recursively via exec_ctx_t, carrying
       target_group (preserved across pipe segments), bg flag, and dep_type.

     Variable expansion (two-phase)
       Phase 1 (collect) — at task creation: validates $VAR references,
       converts them to implicit dependencies, preserves $ text as-is.
       Phase 2 (expand)  — when dependencies resolve: replaces $VAR with
       the referenced task's actual output text, then sends to the LLM.
       Expansion happens after pipe splitting and redirect stripping,
       so any | or > in expanded output is treated as literal text.

     Dependency system
       Tasks form a DAG via dependency edges.  Each task maintains two
       linked lists: deps (waits for) and dependents (waited by).  DFS
       cycle detection runs on every new dependency to prevent deadlocks.

       Dependency types:
         JOIN      (|)       Source output injected into destination's LLM context.
         SEQUENCE  (;)       Destination waits, no output injection.
         PIPE      (reserved) Streaming mode — not yet implemented.

TASK MODEL
     Three-layer Unix-like process model:

     Task     — A conversation sharing history with sibling tasks.
                Like a Unix thread (shared address space).  Each task
                has a unique task_id (t1, t2, ...).

     Group    — An isolated container.  Only the active task outputs.
                Cross-group communication uses | pipe or /task chain.
                Like a Unix process.  Named by leader task's id.

     Session  — Per-session history storage.  Like a Unix login session.
                Contains multiple groups in a linked list.

     Fork     — /task fork <parent> <desc> creates a child in the parent's
                group, inheriting a slice of the parent's conversation.

TASK STATES
       IDLE → WAITING_DEPS → WAITING_INPUT → LLM_PROCESSING
         ⇄ TOOL_CALLING → SUSPENDED → COMPLETED / CANCELLED / ERROR

     LLM_PROCESSING and TOOL_CALLING alternate as tools are invoked.
     Tasks reach COMPLETED on final LLM response; CANCELLED when stopped;
     ERROR on failure.

     - COMPLETED tasks persist until reclaimed with /task join.
     - WAITING_DEPS tasks accept input and queue it until deps resolve.
     - SUSPENDED tasks can be resumed.

SHELL-LIKE JOB CONTROL
     Shell            EdgeClaw
     ─────────────────────────────────────────
     cmd &            task "input" &  or  task "input" --bg
     cmd | cmd        a | b | c  (pipe chain, output injected)
     cmd ; cmd        a ; b      (sequential, no injection)
     cmd > file       task > out.md  (redirect output)
     cmd >> file      task >> log    (append output)
     cmd 2> file      task 2> err    (stderr redirect)
     cmd < file       task < in.txt  (input from file)
     jobs             /jobs  (current group only)
     fg %n            /fg <id>
     bg %n            /bg [id]
     kill %n          /cancel <id>
     Ctrl+Z           /task suspend <id>
     fg / bg (resume) /task resume <id>
     !!   !n          !!  !3  (history expansion)
     $LAST  $1  $$    variable expansion

COMMAND REFERENCE
     Session
     /new          Start a new session.
     /stop         Cancel all active tasks in foreground group.
     /help         Show help message.
     /exit         Exit edgeclaw.
     /whoami       Show current session, model, and group info.
     /model [name] Show or set the current LLM model name.
     /history      Show the last 20 CLI history entries.

     Job control
     /jobs         List tasks in the current foreground group with IDs.
     /tasks        List all tasks in all groups (alias: /task list).
     /cancel <id>  Cancel a task by id.
     /fg <id>      Bring a task to the foreground.
     /bg [id]      Move a task to the background (no arg: current fg).

     Task creation
     /task new <desc>                  Create new group + task (foreground).
     /task new <desc> --bg             Create new group + task (background).
     /task new <desc> -g <gid>         Create in existing group.
     /task new <desc> -d <tid>         Create + wait for dependency.
     /task fork <pid> <desc>           Fork child in parent's group.
     /task fork <pid> <desc> --prompt <name>
                                       Fork with subagent prompt.

     Task inspection
     /task list               List all groups and tasks. Active task
                               marked with * (e.g. [L*] or [M*]).
     /task status [tid]       Show task details (state, output, deps).
     /task output <tid>       Show only the task's output text.
     /task groups             List all process groups.

     Foreground / active
     /task fg <tid>           Foreground + set as active task.
     /task bg                 Move current foreground to background.
     /task switch <tid>       Set active task within foreground group.
                               Only the active task outputs to terminal.

     Task control
     /task stop <tid>         Cancel (SIGTERM — notifies deps).
     /task stop --force <id>  Force-kill (SIGKILL — no dep notification).
     /task stop --group <id>  Cancel all tasks in a process group.
     /task suspend <tid>      Pause a task (Ctrl+Z).
     /task resume <tid>       Resume a suspended task.
     /task join <tid>         Show output, auto-inject into active task
                               (or create one), then reclaim.
     /task prompt <tid> <name>
                               Set subagent prompt.

     Pipelines
     /task chain <src> <dst>  dst waits for src.  When src completes,
                               its user inputs and final output are
                               injected into dst's LLM context.
                               Shortcut: src | dst (shell syntax).

OUTPUT
     Only the session's active task outputs to the terminal.  Other tasks
     complete silently; use /task join to retrieve output.  /task fg and
     /task switch change the active task.

CONTEXT SHARING
     Within a group: all tasks share the full conversation history.

     Cross-group (| pipe or /task chain or /task join): source task's user
     inputs and final output are injected into the destination's LLM context
     as a Q&A-style system message:

         [子任务 analyze (id=1)]:
         Q: help me analyze src/ for bugs
         A: Found 3 performance issues ...

WORKFLOW EXAMPLES
     Simple conversation
           $ edgeclaw
           > help me understand this code
           Welcome! How can I help?

     @ syntax (new group + task)
           > @code "write a Python HTTP server"

     Pipe chain (output injected at each step)
           > scan for vulns | create fix plan | apply patches

     Sequential (no output injection)
           > review src/ ; run tests ; commit changes

     Sequential with redirect
           > review src/ ; run tests ; summarize findings > report.md

     Combined pipes + redirects
           > analyze codebase | find critical bugs | generate report > bugs.md
           > build project 2> build-errors.log
           > fix all issues < context.txt

     Background + pipeline
           > analyze the error logs &
           > audit API endpoints &
           > /jobs
             [1] running  1  "analyze the error logs"   [bg]
             [2] running  2  "audit API endpoints"      [bg]
           > merge both results -d 1 -d 2 > merged.md

     Variable expansion (two-phase)
           > find performance bottlenecks in src/
           ... task 1 completes ...
           > based on $LAST, create an optimization plan
           ... task 2 waits for task 1, then sees its output ...

     History expansion
           > review the latest PR
           > !!                    # repeats "review the latest PR"
           > !3                    # repeats command #3 from history

     Fork with subagent
           > /task fork task-0 "audit the database schema" --prompt dba

     Single command (non-interactive)
           $ edgeclaw -c "check src/main.c for bugs"

     Script file
           $ edgeclaw review.ecs

FILES
     ~/.edgeclaw/                        EdgeClaw home directory.
     ~/.edgeclaw/config.json             Configuration (API key, model).
     ~/.edgeclaw/.cli_history            CLI command history.
     ~/.edgeclaw/workspace/              Workspace root.
     ~/.edgeclaw/workspace/<sid>/        Per-session directory.
     ~/.edgeclaw/workspace/<sid>/history.jsonl  Session history.
     ~/.edgeclaw/workspace/memory/MEMORY.md     Agent memory.
     ~/.edgeclaw/workspace/skills/       Custom skill SKILL.md files.

